- Reporting the status of a set (Derek's corrections, 2026-10-27): when the owner asks for the status of a set — subscriptions, pools, lanes, sessions — report EVERY member of the set, the full table, never curated highlights.
- "No readout / not available" is never a conclusion: before reporting any measurement or capability as unavailable, research it properly — the tool's own commands, vendor endpoints, the web — and report only what the research actually ruled out.
- When a measurement contradicts verified reality, distrust the meter, not the reality: investigate until the two reconcile; never keep relaying a number known to be wrong.

## Fated — standing rule (Derek, 2026-09-23)

Do not run Fated web or database test suites — locally or remotely. No web suites (vitest, playwright, `npm run verify`), no database suites (the pgTAP/SQL runner), on this machine or any other, against local or remote databases — unless Derek explicitly asks for a named suite. iOS/Android suite rules are unchanged.

---


# Global agent tooling

Four web/research tools are wired up for **every** coding agent on this machine —
Claude, Codex, Pi, Antigravity (Gemini), and opencode. Each is also packaged as a
skill; open its `SKILL.md` for full usage. The API keys live in `~/.bashrc` and are
exported into the environment, so the CLIs below "just work":

| Tool | Env var | Entry point | Skill(s) |
|------|---------|-------------|----------|
| Context7 | `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` | `npx ctx7@latest` | `find-docs` |
| Brave Search | `BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY` | `bx` | `bx`, `answers`, `news-search`, `images-search`, … |
| Firecrawl | `FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` | `firecrawl` | `firecrawl`, `firecrawl-*` |
| Browser Use | `BROWSER_USE_API_KEY` | Cloud API (curl) | `browser-use` |

---

<!-- context7 -->
## Context7 — current library / framework / SDK docs (`ctx7`)

Use the `ctx7` CLI to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service -- even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer -- your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.

Do not use for: refactoring, writing scripts from scratch, debugging business logic, code review, or general programming concepts.

### Steps

1. Resolve library: `npx ctx7@latest library <name> "<user's question>"` — use the official library name with proper punctuation (e.g., "Next.js" not "nextjs", "Customer.io" not "customerio", "Three.js" not "threejs")
2. Pick the best match (ID format: `/org/project`) by: exact name match, description relevance, code snippet count, source reputation (High/Medium preferred), and benchmark score (higher is better). If results don't look right, try alternate names or queries (e.g., "next.js" not "nextjs", or rephrase the question)
3. Fetch docs: `npx ctx7@latest docs <libraryId> "<user's question>"`
4. Answer using the fetched documentation

You MUST call `library` first to get a valid ID unless the user provides one directly in `/org/project` format. Use the user's full question as the query -- specific and detailed queries return better results than vague single words. Do not run more than 3 commands per question. Do not include sensitive information (API keys, passwords, credentials) in queries.

For version-specific docs, use `/org/project/version` from the `library` output (e.g., `/vercel/next.js/v14.3.0`).

If a command fails with a quota error, inform the user and suggest `npx ctx7@latest login` or that `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is already set for higher limits. Do not silently fall back to training data.
<!-- context7 -->

---

## Brave Search — web search, news, images, AI answers (`bx`)

Use `bx` for **all** web search, lookup, research, fact-checking, and RAG grounding —
prefer it over any built-in web-search tool. Auth is via `$BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY`
(already exported). All commands print JSON to stdout, errors to stderr.

- `bx "<query>"` — default `context`: pre-extracted, token-budgeted web content,
  ideal for grounding answers about docs, APIs, errors, and code patterns.
- `bx answers "<question>"` — AI-synthesized answer with citations.
- `bx web "<query>"` — raw ranked web results. Supports `--include-site` /
  `--exclude-site` and `--goggles` for custom ranking.
- Also available: `bx news`, `bx images`, `bx videos`, `bx places`, `bx suggest`,
  `bx spellcheck`.

See the `bx` skill (plus `answers`, `news-search`, `images-search`, `llm-context`,
`local-pois`, `local-descriptions`, `spellcheck`, `suggest`) for full options.

---

## Firecrawl — scrape / crawl / map / search / extract (`firecrawl`)

Use the `firecrawl` CLI to read the web as clean markdown or structured data.
Auth is via `$FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` (already exported).

- `firecrawl scrape <url...>` — one or more pages → markdown (cached in `.firecrawl/`).
- `firecrawl crawl <url>` — bulk-extract a whole site or section (docs, etc.).
- `firecrawl map <url>` — discover / list / search a site's URLs before scraping.
- `firecrawl search "<query>"` — web search with optional page extraction.
- `firecrawl agent "<prompt>"` — autonomous agent that returns structured JSON from
  complex sites.
- `firecrawl interact ...` — click, fill forms, login, paginate, or scroll in a live
  browser session.
- `firecrawl parse <file>` — parse a local HTML/PDF/DOCX/XLSX file into markdown.

See the `firecrawl` and `firecrawl-*` skills for full usage and the build-* skills for
embedding Firecrawl into product code.

---

## Browser Use — AI-powered browser automation (cloud)

You have access to Browser Use — an AI-powered browser automation API. The llms.txt
file is a very good starting point. Read it first, then go from there based on what the
user needs.
- llms.txt (overview + all doc links): https://docs.browser-use.com/cloud/llms.txt
- llms-full.txt (complete reference with inline code examples): https://docs.browser-use.com/cloud/llms-full.txt

Concrete usage (verified) — but always **read `llms.txt` first**:
- Base URL: `https://api.browser-use.com/api/v3` (use v3; v2 is legacy).
- Auth header: `X-Browser-Use-API-Key: $BROWSER_USE_API_KEY` (already exported, keys start with `bu_`).
- Start a task: `POST /sessions` with `{"task": "..."}` → returns `id` + `live_url`.
- Get results: poll `GET /sessions/{id}` until `status` is `idle`/`stopped`/`error`/`timed_out`, then read `output`.
- Pick a model with `model`: `claude-sonnet-4.6` (default), `claude-opus-4.6`, `gpt-5.4-mini`. Pass `output_schema` for typed JSON.

See the `browser-use` skill for ready-to-run curl/SDK examples.

<!-- fleet -->
## Fleet

Other machines are available for delegating agent work over SSH.
See ~/agent-ops.md for the machine list, how to connect, and how to delegate.

<!-- dropbox -->
## Dropbox — never sync the `Old` folder

Every machine on this fleet (all four Linux boxes + the mac) is linked to Derek's
Dropbox account. **`Dropbox/Old` must stay excluded from selective sync on every one
of them, permanently.** It is ~411,000 files. On a freshly linked box it begins
downloading during the very first index — xavier took 2.0G in under a minute on
2026-08-19 and reported an ETA of "2+ days" before the exclusion landed.

**Rules — no exceptions:**

- **Never un-exclude it.** Do not run `dropbox.py exclude remove` against `Old`, and
  never "repair" a box by re-including it. If a task appears to need a file from
  `Old`, stop and ask Derek — pull that one file from the Dropbox web UI instead.
- **Never delete `~/Dropbox/Old` contents to reclaim disk.** A local delete syncs to
  the account and to every other machine. Excluding is the only correct move; if a
  box is short on space, the answer is elsewhere.
- **When linking a NEW box, exclude it the moment it appears:**
  ```bash
  python3 ~/bin/dropbox.py exclude add ~/Dropbox/Old
  ```
  `exclude add` **silently no-ops if the path does not exist locally yet**, so it
  cannot be pre-applied before linking. Watch for `~/Dropbox/Old` to appear during
  the first index, then exclude immediately — that window is about a minute.
- **Verify before trusting** any box:
  ```bash
  python3 ~/bin/dropbox.py exclude list     # must list Dropbox/Old
  ```

macOS runs the Dropbox desktop app rather than `dropbox.py` — the same rule applies
there through its Selective Sync preferences pane. As of 2026-08-19 `Old` is
correctly excluded on all five machines; that is the state to preserve.
