## Delegating to the mac (dereks-macbook-air)

**Every Linux box can reach the mac** as of 2026-08-19 — each has its own key
(`xanthe-hub`, `xavier-hub`, `dominic-hub`, `brooklynn-hub`) authorized there and an
identical `Host dereks-macbook-air mac` entry in `/root/.ssh/config`.

**Use the wrapper — it handles every gotcha below:**

```bash
/root/projects/fated/automations/mac-run --check              # is the mac awake? (exit 0 = up)
/root/projects/fated/automations/mac-run '<command>'          # run with PATH fixed up
/root/projects/fated/automations/mac-run --detach '<command>' # long job: nohup + caffeinate
```

Derek's MacBook Air is the **only** machine that can do iOS work — Xcode 26.6 and
the iOS 26.4 simulators live there and nowhere else on the fleet. It is **no longer
the preferred box for Android**: Android development, emulators and Google Play
releases moved to **nibbles** on 2026-08-22 (SDK at `~/Android/Sdk`, JDK 21, `gplay`
at `/usr/local/bin/gplay`, Play service account at `~/.gplay/`, upload keystore at
`~/.android-signing/`). The mac keeps its Android tooling because
`fated-daily-android-production-release` is still `MAC_BOUND=1`. For anything else
prefer a Linux box: it is Derek's
interactive machine, it is often asleep, and it has no tmux.

**Three gotchas that break the normal fleet pattern** (verified 2026-08-19):

1. **User is `derekblair`, not root** — unlike every Linux box.
2. **`bash -lc` does not work, and `zsh -lc` alone is not enough.** The mac's
   non-interactive SSH PATH omits `~/.local/bin` and `~/.npm-global/bin`, so
   `claude`, `flowdeck`, `gemini`, `qwen`, `pi` and `opencode` all come back "not
   found" — only `codex` and `xcodebuild` resolve. Raw form, if you must:
   ```bash
   ssh mac 'zsh -lc '\''export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$HOME/.npm-global/bin:$PATH"; <cmd>'\'''
   ```
3. **There is no tmux on macOS.** The `tmux new -d -s job1 ...` detach convention
   does not apply — use `nohup` plus `caffeinate -i` so the machine does not sleep
   mid-run and kill the job.

Always check reachability first — an asleep mac is the normal case, not a fault. If
it is down, say so and fall back to a Linux box; Tailscale cannot wake it. Treat the
mac as **best-effort, never a dependency** for anything scheduled or unattended.

It is Derek's interactive machine: he may be typing on it. Keep work scoped, never
touch his GUI session, keychain or system settings, and if a task wants sudo there,
stop and ask. The code review gate, PRs-against-`develop` and never-merge rules all
still apply to work done on the mac.
